ttps://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/351006?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Hello71","html_url":"https://github.com/Hello71","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Hello71/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Hello71/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Hello71/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Hello71/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Hello71/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Hello71/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Hello71/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Hello71/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Hello71/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2020-12-25T18:12:11Z","updated_at":"2020-12-25T18:12:11Z","body":"As I see it, there are two solutions here: the simpler solution is to simply call `setsockopt(sock, IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_CORK, &(int){1}, sizeof(int))` before and `setsockopt(sock, IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_CORK, &(int){0}, sizeof(int))` after queuing the nghttp2 messages. However, that involves two additional system calls for each connection. The more complicated solution is to figure out why nghttp2 is immediately calling the send callback, and either pause that, or buffer those messages inside curl before sending them out with writev if available.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/curl/curl/issues/comments/751278326/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"Hello71","id":351006,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM1MTAwNg==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/351006?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Hello71","html_url":"https://github.com/Hello71","followers_url":"h